---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import InclineSim from '../../simulators/incline/InclineSim.astro';
import { inclineQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/incline';

const toc = [
  { id: 'slopes', label: 'Why slopes matter' },
  { id: 'components', label: 'Splitting the weight' },
  { id: 'normal', label: 'The normal force' },
  { id: 'friction', label: 'Static and kinetic friction' },
  { id: 'slip', label: 'When does it slip?' },
  { id: 'sliding', label: 'Sliding down and up' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model assumes' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Ling, S. J., Sanny, J., & Moebs, W. (2016). University Physics Volume 1, Section 6.2: Friction. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-2-friction' },
  { text: 'Ling, S. J., Sanny, J., & Moebs, W. (2016). University Physics Volume 1, Section 6.1: Solving Problems with Newton’s Laws. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-1/pages/6-1-solving-problems-with-newtons-laws' },
  { text: 'Urone, P. P., & Hinrichs, R. (2020). College Physics 2e, Section 5.1: Friction. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/college-physics-2e/pages/5-1-friction' },
  { text: 'Halliday, D., Resnick, R., & Walker, J. (2018). Fundamentals of Physics (11th ed.), Chapter 6: Force and Motion II. Wiley.' },
  { text: 'Persson, B. N. J. (2000). Sliding Friction: Physical Principles and Applications (2nd ed.). Springer.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="physics"
  topic="inclined-planes"
  description="Interactive inclined plane simulator: tilt a slope, drag a block onto it and release it, and watch static friction hold it or kinetic friction slow its slide, with a live free-body diagram, force components and the full calculation."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={inclineQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Put a book on a table and slowly lift one end. For a while nothing happens; then, suddenly, the book slides off. What
      held it, and why did it let go all at once? Tilt the slope, drag the block wherever you like and release it, and watch
      the forces change as static friction holds on and then gives way.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <InclineSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Drag the <strong>block</strong> along the slope and let go: it is released from rest and the simulation starts. Or press <strong>Release from start</strong>.</li>
      <li>Change the angle with the slider or drag the <strong>blue handle</strong> at the top of the slope. Watch the free-body diagram and the friction bar.</li>
      <li>Change μs and μk, or pick a surface. Press <strong>Go to the critical angle</strong> to tilt the slope to the point where the block just slips.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Push up the slope</strong> to give the block a speed of 3 m/s uphill and see friction act downhill.</li>
      <li>Compare the orange block with the grey frictionless one, use <strong>Step</strong> or slow motion to follow the motion, and save runs on the graph to compare settings.</li>
      <li>Tap any part of the picture for an explanation. <strong>Reset</strong> restores every setting.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="slopes">Why slopes matter</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>inclined plane</strong> is a flat surface tilted at an angle θ to the horizontal. Ramps, roads, roofs and
    playground slides are all inclined planes. A slope lets you raise a heavy load with a smaller force, over a longer
    distance, which is why it is counted as one of the six classic simple machines. Studying a block on a slope is also the
    best way to practise splitting forces into components.
  </p>

  <h2 id="components">Splitting the weight</h2>
  <p>
    The weight <strong>W = mg</strong> always points straight down. On a slope it is easier to use axes along and
    perpendicular to the surface, so we split the weight into two <strong>components</strong>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>W∥ = mg sin θ</strong>, along the slope, pulling the block down it.</li>
    <li><strong>W⊥ = mg cos θ</strong>, perpendicular to the slope, pressing the block into it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Here m is the mass in kilograms, g ≈ 9.81 m/s² is the gravitational field strength and θ is the angle of the slope. On
    level ground (θ = 0) W∥ is zero; on a vertical wall (θ = 90°) all of the weight acts along the surface. The two
    components together have exactly the same effect as the weight: they are not extra forces.
  </p>

  <h2 id="normal">The normal force</h2>
  <p>
    The surface pushes back on the block, at right angles to the surface. This is the <strong>normal force N</strong>
    (“normal” means perpendicular). The block does not sink into the slope or float off it, so the forces perpendicular to
    the slope balance: <strong>N = mg cos θ</strong>. On a slope, the normal force is less than the weight, and the steeper
    the slope, the smaller it becomes.
  </p>

  <h2 id="friction">Static and kinetic friction</h2>
  <p>
    Friction acts along the surface and opposes sliding. It comes from microscopic bumps on the two surfaces catching on
    each other and from weak bonds between them. There are two kinds:
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Friction</th><th>When it acts</th><th>Size</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Static</strong></td><td>Surfaces not sliding</td><td>Whatever is needed, up to f<sub>s,max</sub> = μs N</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Kinetic</strong></td><td>Surfaces sliding</td><td>f<sub>k</sub> = μk N, opposing the motion</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    μs and μk are the <strong>coefficients of static and kinetic friction</strong>. They have no units and depend on the
    two materials in contact: around 0.03–0.1 for ice on ice, around 0.3–0.5 for wood on wood and up to about 1 for rubber
    on dry concrete. For the same surfaces, μk is usually smaller than μs: it takes more force to start something sliding
    than to keep it sliding. Static friction is not a fixed value. On a gentle slope it is small, just enough to cancel W∥;
    it grows as the slope steepens, until it reaches its limit.
  </p>

  <h2 id="slip">When does it slip?</h2>
  <p>
    The block stays still as long as static friction can supply the force needed: mg sin θ ≤ μs mg cos θ. The mass cancels,
    leaving <strong>tan θ ≤ μs</strong>. The angle at which the block starts to slide is the <strong>critical angle</strong>
    θc = tan⁻¹(μs). With μs = 0.5, θc ≈ 26.6°, for a 0.5 kg block or a 20 kg one. This gives a neat way to measure μs:
    tilt the surface until the object just slips and measure the angle.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sliding">Sliding down and up</h2>
  <p>
    Once the block slides, kinetic friction takes over. Taking “down the slope” as positive, Newton’s second law along the
    slope gives
  </p>
  <p><strong>ma = mg sin θ − μk mg cos θ, so a = g (sin θ − μk cos θ)</strong></p>
  <p>
    Again the mass cancels, so heavy and light blocks of the same material slide together. On a frictionless slope this
    becomes a = g sin θ. If the block is pushed <em>up</em> the slope, friction now points down the slope, alongside W∥, so
    it slows at g (sin θ + μk cos θ). When it stops, static friction takes over again: it either holds the block or, if the
    slope is steeper than θc, the block starts to slide back down. Because μk is less than μs, a block that has just
    slipped at the critical angle accelerates rather than creeping.
  </p>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Roads and ramps</strong>: wheelchair ramps have gentle slopes (about 5°) so little force is needed, and steep roads may need gritting in winter, when μ falls.</li>
    <li><strong>Avalanches and landslides</strong>: snow or soil stays put until the slope or the loading exceeds what friction can hold, then gives way suddenly.</li>
    <li><strong>Car brakes and tyres</strong>: anti-lock brakes keep tyres rolling instead of skidding, using static friction, which is larger than kinetic friction.</li>
    <li><strong>Conveyors and chutes</strong>: engineers choose angles and surfaces so that parcels slide, or stay put, as needed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“The normal force always equals the weight.”</strong> On a slope it is mg cos θ, less than mg.</li>
    <li><strong>“Static friction is always μs N.”</strong> That is only its maximum; it matches whatever force it is resisting.</li>
    <li><strong>“Heavier blocks slide down faster.”</strong> With the same surfaces, the acceleration does not depend on mass.</li>
    <li><strong>“Friction always points up the slope.”</strong> It opposes sliding, so it points down the slope when the block moves up.</li>
    <li><strong>“The components of the weight are extra forces.”</strong> They replace the weight; they are not added to it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model assumes</h2>
  <p>
    The block is treated as a single point: it slides but cannot tip over or roll. Friction follows the simple
    (Coulomb) model, in which μs and μk are constants that do not depend on speed or contact area; real friction varies
    with speed, temperature and surface condition. There is no air resistance, the slope and block are perfectly rigid,
    and g = 9.81 m/s². The block is released from rest unless you push it, and it stops against a stop at the bottom of the
    slope. The block drawing grows with mass only to make the change visible. The arrows are drawn to scale relative to
    the weight.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>On a slope, split the weight into W∥ = mg sin θ (along) and W⊥ = mg cos θ (into the slope).</li>
    <li>The normal force balances W⊥: N = mg cos θ.</li>
    <li>Static friction matches the force it resists, up to μs N; kinetic friction is μk N and opposes sliding.</li>
    <li>A block slips when tan θ &gt; μs; the critical angle θc = tan⁻¹(μs) does not depend on mass.</li>
    <li>Sliding down, a = g (sin θ − μk cos θ); on a frictionless slope, a = g sin θ.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
